Chapter: GpuPanelBorder

  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites UI chapter Theme
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter BoxModel
  cites UI chapter GpuRender

 A panel must have a visible extent, and on the GPU backend it had none.

 Every built-in theme gives its panel style a background equal to the page
 background and a one-pixel border, so the border is the only thing that
 delineates a panel at all. The CPU renderer draws it -- render-panel calls
 render-border, and so do the gauge, button and input paths. The GPU
 renderer emitted the fill and dropped the border, so on the backend every
 bare-metal app actually uses, a panel painted its parent's colour onto its
 parent and disappeared. That is why the GuiOS sidebar and taskbar had no
 extent while the depth rule was correct and the triangle count was healthy:
 the geometry was submitted, it was in front, and it was the same colour as
 what it covered.

 Neither the triangle count nor gpu-depth-tree can see this. Both backends
 agreed on the fill, so a rect was always emitted; what differed was one the
 theme asked for and only one backend drew. The instrument has to read
 pixels and it has to look for the border colour specifically.

 The probe tree carries no separator, which is the whole reason it is shaped
 this way: a separator paints pal-border too, so its presence would make this
 count non-zero whatever the panel does. With it absent, pal-border in the
 framebuffer can only have come from a panel border. Run against a renderer
 without the fix the count is zero.

 The scan strides eight columns and every row. A border is one pixel wide, so
 a column stride crosses the vertical edges and a row stride could step over
 the horizontal ones. Counting rather than sampling a computed coordinate
 keeps the test honest when layout moves.

Section: Body

  fb-base : Integer = #BF000000
  fb-w : Integer = 640
  fb-h : Integer = 480

  scan-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  scan-row (y) (x) (target) (acc) =
    if x >= fb-w then acc
    else scan-row y (x + 8) target (acc + (if peek-32 fb-base ((y * fb-w + x) * 4) == target then 1 else 0))

  scan-fb : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  scan-fb (y) (target) (acc) =
    if y >= fb-h then acc
    else scan-fb (y + 1) target (acc + scan-row y 0 target 0)

  probe-tree : WidgetNode
  probe-tree =
    widget-panel "root" DirColumn 4 [
      widget-set-min (widget-panel "inner" DirColumn 0 [
        widget-label "text" "panel"
      ]) 0 60
    ]

  opening : [Gpu, Console] Nothing =
    act
      let th = theme-terminal
      in let pal = th.th-palette
      in let laid = widget-layout probe-tree th (layout-rect 0 0 fb-w fb-h)
      in act
        c1 <- gr-clear (pal.pal-bg)
        c2 <- gr-clear-depth
        n <- gr-flush ((gr-render-tree (gf-new 4096) laid th gr-depth-scene).gf-idx)
        print-line-uni ("panel-border: " & show (if scan-fb 0 (pal.pal-border) 0 > 0 then 1 else 0))
      end
    end

